# BILLBOARD
1. Prompt Claude (or LLM of your choice) to scrape Wikipedia's Billboard Hot 100 #1 pages for the years 1964-1984.

Here are our original prompts:
"Please scrape the table at this link: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Billboard_Hot_100_number_ones_of_1964 for the 
date, song title, and artist of each Billboard Hot 100 number 1. Put this result into a csv file. Filter the results to 
only include songs by the artist "The Beatles". Remember that one song title can belong to multiple different days, and 
that one artist can belong to multiple different song titles."

"Perfect. Please repeat the same process for the years 1965-1984. In addition to the Beatles, include musical artists John 
Lennon, Paul McCartney, Ringo Starr, and George Harrison."

2. Cross-validate LLM produced output with original Wikipedia page. Especially check for the correct dates, as Claude gets
confused by comma-delimited dates with this data. 

### Code for steps 3-6 are included in the "Billboard_DataCleaning_Validation.Rmd" document

3. Read the resulting .csv into R, checking for nulls and trimming the data to the original 102 row length. 

4. Reformat the data using lubridate and dplyr to create a cumulative data set (how many weeks each song charted for)

5. Validate the data using pointblank to check that variable formats are correct, numeric values are in the correct ranges, 
and that all rows have date columns.

6. Create a distribution with ggplot to visually check for any incorrect data. 


